From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:51:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: max17042_battery: wait out the MAX77759's
 alert

The MAX77759's gauge keeps ALRT asserted until its next task period
after Status is cleared, up to 351 ms with the TaskPeriod its battery
models set. The interrupt is level-low and oneshot, so as soon as the
thread returns it runs again, and each alert becomes a burst: on a
Pixel 7, every 1 % step of RepSOC fired the gauge's interrupt about 420
times within half a second, with Status already clear on each later
run.

Sleep one task period at the end of the thread on the MAX77759, before
the interrupt is unmasked. Google's driver for the same gauge does the
same (max1720x_fg_irq_thread_fn() sleeps 500 ms, "gauge will take up to
351 ms to clear ALRM1"). Alerts arriving meanwhile are kept by the
level interrupt and handled on the next run.

Tested on a Pixel 7 (7.3.0-rc6) with its battery model loaded: before
this change, unplugged and discharging, three 1 % steps of RepSOC fired
the interrupt 424, 429 and 453 times. With it, five alerts in 18
minutes (RepSOC reaching 90 % on the cable, then four steps down to
86 % unplugged) fired it once each, the thresholds moved with each step,
and the gauge reported as before.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
index 78482ef18..e40540228 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
@@ -1237,6 +1237,16 @@ static irqreturn_t max17042_thread_handler(int id, void *dev)
 			  0xFFFF & ~(STATUS_POR_BIT | STATUS_BST_BIT));
 
 	power_supply_changed(chip->battery);
+
+	/*
+	 * The MAX77759 releases ALRT only at its next task period after
+	 * Status is cleared, so the level interrupt would fire again as soon
+	 * as the oneshot thread returns, hundreds of times per alert. Wait
+	 * one task period (in units of 1/32768 s) before returning.
+	 */
+	if (chip->chip_type == MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX77759)
+		msleep(DIV_ROUND_UP(chip->task_period * MSEC_PER_SEC, 32768));
+
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
